That's James Delgado. I'm an archaeologist and a historian and I'm the author of Gold Rush Port, the Maritime Archaeology of San Francisco. Delgado was 20 years old then, an archaeologist in training, and he was part of the excavation that day. Inside the ship, he found all kinds of artifacts from the gold rush that had been preserved underground. Bricks, nails, tin plates for sheathing, boots, jackets, saddles, shovels, a lot of booze, 49ers like to drink. Delgado even popped the cork on a
那是詹姆斯·德尔加多。我是考古学家兼历史学家，也是《淘金热港口：旧金山海洋考古学》一书的作者。当时的德尔加多只有20岁，是一名见习考古学家，他参与了当天的发掘工作。在船舱内部，他发现了各种地下保存下来的淘金热时期的文物。有砖块、钉子、用于船身包覆的锡板、靴子、夹克、马鞍、铁锹、还有大量的酒——49人淘金者们很爱喝酒。德尔加多甚至还撬开了一瓶

4
00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:04.000
bottle of 150 year old champagne. Tasted awful. But the excavation of the Niantic launched his career in marine archeology and inspired him to study the ships beneath San Francisco, because the Niantic isn't the only one down there. I'd say that there are probably as many as 70 ships that were buried that in some way
150年前的香槟酒瓶的软木塞。味道糟透了。但“尼亚加拉号”的发掘开启了他的海洋考古生涯，并激励他去研究旧金山地下的船只，因为“尼亚加拉号”并不是那下面唯一的船。我说过，那下面可能有多达70艘船被埋藏，它们以某种

5
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:42.000
shape-reform remained even if it's fragments. In 1994, city workers were digging a tunnel for the munitrain when they hit the side of another gold rush ship, the Rome, and the decision was made to drill through it. They tunneled right through the hall, and today thousands of San Francisco's travel through the ship every morning as they'd ride the train between Folsom Street and the Embarcadero. It's not really marked in the tunnel, but for those of us who know when you take that turn, you're passing through the Gold Rush Ship Room. To understand how all of these ships ended up buried underground beneath the city, we
形式和形态残留着，哪怕只剩下残片。1994年，城市工人在为城市轨道交通挖隧道时，撞到了另一艘淘金热时期的船只——“罗马号”的船舷，当时决定直接钻过去。他们直接在船体上凿出了隧道。今天，数千名旧金山人在福尔松街和恩巴卡德罗之间乘车时，每天早晨都会穿过这艘船。隧道里虽然没有明显的标记，但对于我们这些知情的人来说，当你转过那个弯时，你其实正穿过淘金热时期的船只——“罗马号”。为了理解所有这些船是如何最终被埋在城市地表之下的，我们需要

