00:02:42.000 --> 00:03:17.000
need to go back to the 1830s. When San Francisco was just a tiny little port, called Yerba Boena. It was an outpost of a few buildings with a tiny town square that set. By 1847 the population had grown to a few hundred. They renamed the port San Francisco, but it was still just a small town. But in 1848, all of that changed with the discovery of gold in January. The gold rush brought a tidal wave of prospectors to California. And by the end of 1850, the San Francisco waterfront was clogged with more than a thousand ships and the town had grown into a city of
回到19世纪30年代。当时的旧金山还只是一个小得不能再小、名叫耶尔巴布埃纳的港口。它是一个只有几栋建筑物和一个小小城镇广场的前哨站。到了1847年，人口增长到了几百人。他们将港口改名为旧金山，但它依然只是个小镇。但在1848年，随着1月份黄金的发现，这一切都改变了。淘金热给加利福尼亚带来了一波又一波的淘金者。到1850年底，旧金山的海岸线上挤满了1000多艘船，小镇也发展成了一座拥有

7
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:49.000
thousands. So you're looking at a city that within a decade has gone from being a village to being the 12th largest city in North America. But the land around San Francisco was mostly sandy hills and not well suited to the scale of development. So to meet the surging demand for real estate, the city sold off plots of water in the shallow tidal zone. A person could buy a piece of water, but it was their job to turn it into usable ground. Some people dumped sand or garbage into their plots to try and make land.
数千人口的城市。所以，你看到的是一座在十年内从一个小村庄变成了北美第十二大城市的城市。但是旧金山周围的土地大多是沙丘，并不适合大规模开发。因此，为了满足对房地产飙升的需求，这座城市拍卖了浅水潮汐区的水域地块。一个人可以买下一片水域，但把这片水域变成可用土地是他们自己的事。有些人往自己的地块里倾倒沙子或垃圾，试图造出陆地。

8
00:03:49.000 --> 00:04:18.000
Others drove steaks into the mud and built platforms on top of the water. And a lot of people repurposed the ships that had brought them to California. The ships helped, because as they came, many of them were sitting idle. The crews have gone off to the diggings or had run away. So the owners rented them out or sold them outright to become floating houses, square houses, the town jail, offices. You name it. Over 200 of them became floating buildings.
还有些人在泥地里打桩，在水面上搭建平台。许多人还重新利用了带他们来到加利福尼亚的船只。这些船帮了大忙，因为当它们到达时，许多都处于闲置状态。船员们已经去淘金了或者逃跑了。因此，船主们把它们租出去或直接卖掉，用来做水上住宅、简易房、镇上的监狱、办公室，应有尽有。其中有200多艘变成了水上建筑。

9
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:33.000
Ships were the centers of commercial life in San Francisco. Well, you had the Apollo saloon, which was basically a restaurant. They sold the donut and a cup of coffee for two bits, they said. You had the euphemia, which was turned into the town jail, and later became an insane asylum.
船只曾是旧金山商业生活的中心。比如阿波罗沙龙，它基本上是一家餐馆。据说他们卖一个甜甜圈加一杯咖啡只要两毛五。还有“尤菲米娅号”，它被改成了镇上的监狱，后来又变成了一家疯人院。

10
00:04:33.000 --> 00:05:10.000
You also had ships that served as water sisters. You had other ships that worked as government offices. And another ship, the good ship Panama, was used as a Siemens church. The city looked like a forest of masts, and planks connected the ships, creating a grid of boardwalks perched above the water. One visitor said that San Francisco was a Venice built of pine. But there were obvious downsides to a floating city, and little by little, people began to fill in areas between the ships using whatever they could get their hands on.
还有一些船被用作水库船。还有其他船作为政府办公地点。另一艘船“优秀的巴拿马号”则被用作海员教堂。这座城市看起来就像一片由桅杆组成的森林，木板将船只连接起来，在水面上方交织成一个木板路网。一位游客曾说，旧金山是一座用松木建造的威尼斯。但水上城市也有明显的弊端，人们开始一点一点地用手头能找到的任何东西填平船只之间的区域。

11
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:56.000
From garbage to unwanted cargo to dead bodies, you name it, everything was thrown into that, and with this gradually began the process of filling in the mud flats. In this way, the shoreline itself began to advance. At this point, the harbor was a patchwork of freshly filled lots, ships, and buildings on stills. But then, in May of 1851, a massive fire broke out in downtown San Francisco. The wind fan the flames, and by the time the next day had dawned, more than 2,000 buildings were gone, the flames were so high that they could see the glow in Monterey 100 miles south. Some of the ships caught fire and sank, leaving their half-charred holes in the muddy water.
从垃圾、不需要的货物到尸体，应有尽有，所有东西都被扔进里面，泥滩填埋的过程也由此逐渐展开。通过这种方式，海岸线本身开始向前推进。此时，港口是由新填筑的地块、船只和高脚屋建筑拼凑而成的。然而，在1851年5月，旧金山市中心爆发了一场大火。风助火势，到了第二天黎明时分，2000多栋建筑消失了，火势之猛烈，远在100英里以南的蒙特雷都能看到火光。一些船只起火并沉没，在泥泞的水面上留下了半焦黑的船体。

12
00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:20.000
By the time they managed to put the fire out, the waterfront was just a mushy mess of smoldering timber. And that mushy mess was too unstable to rebuild on, so they used some of the world's first steam-powered excavators to scrape sand off of nearby hills and dunes and dump it on top. By September of 1851, the old waterfront had been completely buried.
当人们终于扑灭大火时，海滨只剩下一片冒烟木材的糊状烂摊子。而这片糊状烂摊子太不稳定，无法在其上重建，于是他们动用了世界上最早的一些蒸汽挖掘机，从附近的山丘和沙丘上刮下沙子，倾倒在上面。到1851年9月，旧的海滨已经完全被埋葬了。

13
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:56.000
And with it, the nianctic, the Rome, and all of the other gold rush ships entombed in this manufactured ground. After the fire, San Francisco continued to expand, filling in more and more of the tidal zone in deeper and deeper water. And eventually the state stepped in. They built a seawall to cap the production of new land. They drew a line and said San Francisco you can go no further, and today we know that line, it's the Embarcadero. The walkway along the eastern edge of the San Francisco Wharf. But by that time a city had built up on top of all this sand and dirt and garbage.
随之一起被埋葬的，还有“尼亚加拉号”、“罗马号”以及所有其他被封印在人工填造土地底下的淘金船。大火过后，旧金山继续扩张，在更深的水域填海造地，填满了越来越多的潮汐区。最终政府介入了。他们修建了一道海堤来限制新土地的开发。他们画了一条线，对旧金山说：不能再往前了。今天我们都知道这条线，那就是恩巴卡德罗——旧金山码头东缘的步行道。但到那时，一座城市已经在这些沙子、泥土和垃圾堆上拔地而起。

14
00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:37.000
And over the years San Francisco continued to develop, and today what had been the title zone is the heart of the city's financial district. Throughout history, the absence of ground has proven to be a surprisingly small obstacle for people who want to build stuff. Simpsisco isn't the only city that is built its own land. Large portions of Boston, Seattle, Hong Kong, and Marseille were built on top of Phil. It's not uncommon to see it in other cities, New York, Manhattan built out and filled. The area of the World Trade Center sits on with landfill.
多年来，旧金山不断发展，如今曾经的潮汐带已成为该市金融区的核心。纵观历史事实证明，对于想要搞建设的人来说，没有土地并不是一个多大的障碍。旧金山并不是唯一一个自己造地的城市。波士顿、西雅图、香港和马赛的大片土地都是建立在填海造地之上的。这在其他城市并不罕见，纽约曼哈顿的土地也是通过填海向外扩展的。世界贸易中心所在的区域就是用垃圾填海造出来的。

15
00:07:37.000 --> 00:08:20.000
They actually found a ship when they were building the original World Trade Center towers. What is now mum-by-India used to be a seven island archipelago before the British colonial government turned it all into one contiguous strip of land. Tokyo has built 60,000 acres of new land over the last several centuries. The process of building new land along the waterfront is often referred to as land reclamation. Which is kind of ridiculous. Land reclamation is another one of those words that's made up and it masquerades what's really happening. You're creating land where it didn't exist before. Or even creating a whole country, like the Netherlands.
事实上，他们在建造最初的世界贸易中心双子塔时就发现了一艘船。如今的孟买（印度），在英国殖民统治者将其变成一个连贯的狭长地带之前，曾是一个由七个岛屿组成的群岛。在过去的几个世纪里，东京已经造了6万英亩的新土地。这种沿着海岸线建造新土地的过程通常被称为填海造陆。这其实挺荒谬的。填海造陆是另一个伪装了真实情况的虚构词汇。你明明是在原本不存在的地方创造土地，甚至是创造一个国家，比如荷兰。

16
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:53.000
There's a very extraordinary history of basically the Dutch making their own country. That's Stephen Graham, professor of cities and society at Newcastle University, and author of the book Vertical, the city from satellites to bunkers. I mean, most of Holland, most of the Netherlands, as it's currently constructed, is an engineering construction. It's a huge project of land reclamation and land manufacture. The Dutch developed incredible techniques to build land in places that were actually below sea level.
荷兰人自己造国的历史非常非凡。说话的是斯蒂芬·格雷姆，纽卡斯尔大学城市与社会学教授，著有《垂直：从卫星到掩体的城市》一书。我的意思是，荷兰的大部分地区，就像它现在的结构一样，是一项工程建设。这是一个巨大的填海造陆和人造土地项目。荷兰人开发了令人难以置信的技术，在实际低于海平面的地方建造土地。

17
00:08:53.000 --> 00:09:20.000
And this has been going on since medieval times, where large dikes were constructed, large pumping systems were constructed to create a dry country underneath the sea. And the Dutch are proud of this history. There's a popular saying there, God created the world, but the Dutch created the Netherlands. Humans have been making their own ground for a long time, but the process has reached new heights in recent years.
这种情况从中世纪就开始了，当时人们修建了大堤，建造了大型抽水系统，在海平面以下创造出了一个干燥的国家。荷兰人为这段历史感到自豪。当地有一句流行语：上帝创造了世界，但荷兰人创造了荷兰。人类制造自己的土地已经有很长时间了，但在近年来，这个过程达到了新的高度。

18
00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:58.000
The historic scale of land reclamation, and land manufacture, and the building up of land beneath cities is absolutely minuscule compared to the scale at which it's going on today. More and more cities are building waterfront property for airports, skyscrapers and housing. It's sheer urban economics because you create new supply in an area where there's amazing levels of demand and therefore it's very profitable. And new dredging technologies have enabled developers to build land faster and on a grander scale, often at the expense of the environment.
与今天的发展规模相比，历史上填海造陆、人造土地以及在城市地下堆砌土地的规模简直微不足道。越来越多的城市为了机场、摩天大楼和住宅而建造滨水地产。这纯粹是城市经济学，因为你在一个需求惊人的地区创造了新的供应，因此利润非常丰厚。新的疏浚技术使开发商能够以更快的速度、更大的规模建设土地，这往往是以牺牲环境为代价的。

19
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:38.000
I mean they use huge ships for example, which have what they call rainbow dredging systems. These ships suck up massive amounts of sand and gravel from the sea floor and shoot it through the air in a giant, arcing rainbow of muck onto the area where they're trying to build new land. This technology has been particularly useful for creating artificial islands. China is using these rainbow dredging ships to turn uninhabited coral atolls in the middle of the South China Sea into larger islands that could support human settlement. They're actually manufacturing land in the hopes of staking territorial claim to highly contested waters. And then, there's Dubai.
我的意思是，他们使用巨大的船只，例如拥有所谓“彩虹疏浚系统”的船只。这些船只从海底吸走大量的沙子和砾石，然后通过巨大的、呈拱形的泥浆彩虹将其喷射到他们试图建造新土地的区域上。这项技术对于建造人工岛屿特别有用。中国正利用这些彩虹挖泥船，将南海中间无人居住的环礁变成能够支持人类定居的更大岛屿。他们实际上是在制造土地，以期对极具争议的水域提出领土主张。然后，还有迪拜。

20
00:10:38.000 --> 00:11:13.000
Dubai is a post to child for this process really. The wealthy desert Emirate has built a series of artificial islands for wealthy elites. Three of the new landforms are shaped like massive palm fronds. Another project called The World is a cluster of islands designed to look like a map of the world from above. But Dubai's reclamation project has stirred up so much sediment that they buried coral reefs, oyster beds, and sea grass fields in the Persian Gulf, and some of the islands that make up the world are already starting to erode back into the sea.
迪拜可以说是这个过程的典型代表。这个富裕的沙漠酋长国为富有的精英阶层建造了一系列人工岛屿。其中三个新地貌的形状像巨大的棕榈树叶。另一个名为“世界”的项目是一组岛屿，从上方看设计成世界地图的形状。但迪拜的填海项目激起了如此多的沉积物，以至于它们掩埋了波斯湾的珊瑚礁、牡蛎床和海草田，组成“世界”岛屿的一些岛屿已经开始侵蚀回海中。

21
00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:55.000
Dubai is also running out of sand. At least the kind of sand you need to build things. Desert sand is too fine for construction, and Dubai has used up so much of the sand around its coast that they now have to import it from Australia. Globally, humans use over 40 billion tons of sand a year. The two largest uses of that sand are concrete production and land manufacturing projects. And it's created a global sand shortage that is forcing countries to go to extreme lengths in their efforts to build new ground. The most famous example here is Singapore. The wealthy island nation is one of the densest countries in the world.
迪拜的沙子也快用完了。至少是建造东西所需的这种沙子。沙漠沙太细，不适合建筑，迪拜已经用完了海岸周边的沙子，现在不得不从澳大利亚进口。在全球范围内，人类每年消耗超过400亿吨沙子。沙子的两大用途是混凝土生产和人造土地项目。这造成了全球沙子短缺，迫使各国在努力建造新土地时采取极端手段。最著名的例子就是新加坡。这个富裕的岛国是世界上人口最密集的国家之一。

22
00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:22.000
And it's constantly looking to expand its territory. There's plans to make Singapore 20 or 30% bigger over the next 40 or 50 years through manufacturing new land. Without enough sand of its own, Singapore has had to look elsewhere. There's been a lot of evidence that the extended land manufacturing around Singapore, the basis for its growth as a tiny island city, is based on basically stealing sandy islands
它一直在寻求扩大领土。计划在未来40到50年内，通过制造新土地，将新加坡的面积扩大20%或30%。由于自己的沙子不够，新加坡不得不另寻他路。有大量证据表明，新加坡周围不断扩大的人造土地——也就是这个小岛城市增长的基础——基本上是从柬埔寨、印度尼西亚和缅甸等较贫穷国家“偷走”沙质岛屿，

23
00:12:22.000 --> 00:13:08.000
from the poorer countries like Cambodia and Indonesia and Burma and then moving the material physically. Land itself is flowing from poorer countries to wealthier ones. Those countries have made sand exports illegal because of this sense that they are losing their own territory. Land manufacturing isn't just expanding territory outward, in some cases it's expanding upward. On the coast of Nigeria, developers are building a new luxury city called Eco-Atlantic, on top of manufactured ground. And the starting thing about Eco-Atlantic is it's about three or four meters higher than
然后将这些材料物理搬运过去的。土地本身正从较贫穷的国家流向较富裕的国家。由于意识到自己正在失去领土，这些国家已将沙子出口定为非法。造地不仅是在向外扩展领土，在某些情况下它还在向上扩展。在尼日利亚海岸，开发商正在用人造地基建造一个名为“Eko Atlantic”的新豪华城市。关于“Eko Atlantic”，最惊人的一点是它比拉各斯其余地区

